### Title
Package version ordering in conda search output.

### Why
When sharded repodata is enabled, `conda search` displayed package versions in lexicographic string order rather than `VersionOrder` (e.g., `1.11.5` before `1.3.0`).

### Platforms
- [x] Windows
- [ ] macOS
- [ ] Linux

### Prerequisites
Internet access to default channels or access to a channel serving sharded repodata.

### Steps
1. Execute `conda search maturin --override-channels -c defaults`.
2. Inspect the unique sequence of version numbers displayed in the `Version` column.
3. Compare the relative position of `1.3.0` and `1.11.5`.

### Pass criteria
Versions are sorted according to Conda version semantics (`VersionOrder`): `0.14.17` precedes `1.3.0`, `1.3.0` precedes `1.9.4`, and `1.9.4` precedes `1.11.5`. Single-digit minor versions appear before double-digit minor versions with the same major version.

### Out of scope / notes
Non-standard PEP 440 prerelease parsing edge cases (covered by #11721).
